; NDPARTB.DSF - SP11 Task 6 two-part fixture, part 2 (staged as
; GAME2.DDB + PART2\0.XMB by tests\build-tests.ps1 -Part). Paired with
; NDPARTA.DSF (part A, game root) - see that file's header comment for
; the full owner leg: exact commands, expected screen sequence (four
; switches: EXTERN forward/back, LOAD forward auto-switch, RAMLOAD back
; auto-switch), and the optional -Title variant. Not part of the
; automated condacts.dsf suite.
;
; PRO 0 has two branches, told apart by flag 103 on entry: 0 means
; "just arrived from part A live" (the FIRST ENTRY branch below), 1
; means "just arrived via a restored cross-part SAVE" (the XLOAD
; RE-ENTRY branch - only LOADing a save with 103=1 on it produces this,
; since 103 is set to 1 only by this file's own FIRST ENTRY, right
; before the save that carries it is written).

/CTL
_

/VOC
BENCH 50 noun
KEY   51 noun
CHEST 52 noun

/STX
/0  "It's too dark to see anything."
/1  "I can also see:"
/2  "What now?"
/3  "What next?"
/4  "What should I do now?"
/5  "What should I do next?"
/6  "I didn't understand."
/7  "I can't go in that direction."
/8  "I can't do that."
/9  "I have with me:"
/10 "I am wearing:"
/11 " "
/12 "Are you sure?"
/13 "Another go?"
/14 " "
/15 "OK."
/16 "Press any key."
/17 " "
/18 " "
/19 " "
/20 " "
/21 " "
/22 " "
/23 "I'm not wearing one of those."
/24 "I can't. My hands are full."
/25 "I already have the _."
/26 "There isn't one of those here."
/27 "I can't carry any more things."
/28 "I don't have one of those."
/29 "I'm already wearing the _."
/30 "Y"
/31 "N"
/32 "More..."
/33 ">"
/34 " "
/35 " "
/36 "I now have the _."
/37 "I'm now wearing the _."
/38 "I've removed the _."
/39 "I've dropped the _."
/40 "I can't wear the _."
/41 "I can't remove the _."
/42 "I can't remove the _. My hands are full."
/43 "The _ weighs too much for me."
/44 "The _ is in the"
/45 "The _ isn't in the"
/46 ", "
/47 " and "
/48 ".#n"
/49 "I don't have the _."
/50 "I'm not wearing the _."
/51 "."
/52 "There isn't one of those in the"
/53 "Nothing."
/54 " "
/55 " "
/56 " "
/57 "I/O Error"
/58 " "
/59 "File name error."
/60 "Type in name of file."

/MTX

/OTX
/0 "a wooden bench"
/1 "a small brass key"
/2 "a heavy chest"

/LTX
/0 "Part two arena."

/CON
/0

/OBJ
;obj  starts  weight    c w  5 4 3 2 1 0 9 8 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0    noun   adjective
/0    0           1     _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _    BENCH  _
/1    0           1     _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _    KEY    _
/2    0           5     _ _  _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _ _    CHEST  _

/PRO 0
; --- FIRST ENTRY branch (flag 103 = 0: freshly carried from part A
; live). Entries 1-8 below are ALL gated EQ 103 0 (or reached only via
; a gated entry's own fallthrough) so that none of them can also fire
; during the XLOAD RE-ENTRY pass, where 103 is already 1 on arrival.

; Entry 1: carry-through proof. Flags 100/101 and object 1's CARRIED
; location all arrived from part A verbatim - all 256 flags copy across
; a switch, and object locations copy by index for as many objects as
; both parts define in common (see authoring-kit\SETUP.md's multi-part
; "Author caveats" for the full rules a real game must follow).
> _ _   EQ 103 0
        EQ 100 77
        EQ 101 88
        CARRIED 1
        MES "PB CARRY OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   EQ 103 0
        MES "PB CARRY F#n"

; Entry 3: PARTn asset probe proof. Part A's own 0.XMB is staged at the
; SD root (sd\0.XMB); part B's is staged separately at sd\PART2\0.XMB,
; with DIFFERENT bytes at the same internal offset. XMES only prints
; this clean sentence if ext_xmes's PARTn\ probe (SP11 Task 5) actually
; won the race against the root file - a probe that wrongly fell
; through to the root 0.XMB would read A's bytes at B's offset instead,
; producing garbled or simply wrong text, not a crash and not silence.
> _ _   EQ 103 0
        XMES "part two external text via PART2"
        NEWLINE

; Entry 4: SAV round-trip GATE. A standalone gate, separate from its
; own functional body (entry 6), specifically because the body sets
; flag 103 to 1 (LET 103 1, needed so the save it writes carries
; 103=1) BEFORE it can fail (SAVE/LOAD are condition-typed - a failure
; aborts the entry immediately). That makes flag 103 alone ambiguous
; AFTER the body has started: both "the body ran and SAVE/LOAD failed"
; and "we are actually in the XLOAD RE-ENTRY, and this gate correctly
; excluded itself" leave 103=1 behind. Splitting the gate out and
; routing ITS OWN failure straight to $xload (entry 5, below) - instead
; of falling into the body/its sibling/the return leg - resolves the
; ambiguity structurally: entry 7 ("PB SAV F") is then reachable ONLY
; from the body's own internal failure, never from this gate's.
> _ _   EQ 103 0
        SKIP 1
> _ _   SKIP $xload

; Entry 6: SAV round trip body (reached only via entry 4's SKIP 1
; above). Same-part v2 round trip while curPart = 2 - the first place
; in the whole test corpus that exercises h_save/h_load away from
; curPart = 1 (condacts.dsf's own SAVE/LOAD checks always run at part
; 1). LET 103 1 marks this save as "already through part B once" so
; the eventual cross-part reload of THIS SAME FILE (NDPARTA.DSF's own
; LOAD 0) lands back here at the XLOAD RE-ENTRY branch, not this one.
; Type any 1-8 character name, then the SAME name again at the second
; prompt - and the SAME name again for NDPARTA.DSF's own LOAD later
; (one file, one name, three prompts total).
> _ _   MES "PB TYPE NAME: pt#n"
        LET 103 1
        SAVE 0
        MES "PB TYPE NAME: pt#n"
        LOAD 0
        MES "PB SAV OK#n"
        SKIP 1
> _ _   MES "PB SAV F#n"

; Entry 8: the return leg, mirroring part A's own outbound switch
; (NDPARTA.DSF entry 1) - fail-silent on a missing GAME.DDB, same idiom
; as condacts.dsf check 44.
> _ _   EXTERN 1 4
        MES "PB SWITCH F#n"

$xload
; --- XLOAD RE-ENTRY branch (flag 103 = 1: only a restored cross-part
; save produces this on a fresh PRO 0 entry - the FIRST ENTRY branch
; above is the only place that ever sets 103, and only right before
; writing the save that carries it).

; Entry 9: re-assert the carry proof (this state arrived via a save
; file, not a live switch, so it is worth re-checking, not just
; assumed), then RAMLOAD the part-1 buffer NDPARTA.DSF's entry 1 took
; (a cross-part restore - curPart is 2 here, the buffer's own stored
; part is 1) - this auto-switches back to part 1. RAMLOAD is action-
; typed, so on success this entry never finishes (mirrors EXTERN/LOAD's
; own "success never returns" contract); "PB RAMLOAD F" runs only if
; that probe failed.
> _ _   EQ 103 1
        EQ 100 77
        EQ 101 88
        MES "PB XLOAD OK#n"
        RAMLOAD 255
        MES "PB RAMLOAD F#n"

; Entry 10: XLOAD RE-ENTRY carry-check failure witness (sibling to
; entry 9, gated the same way for the same reason as entries 1-8's own
; EQ 103 0 gates).
> _ _   EQ 103 1
        MES "PB XLOAD F#n"
/END
